 we throw at s can be  almost any upper bound on  the square of the log of our confidence  so  this has a sort of remarkable  implication that we can get an algorithm  that simultaneously works for many  different confidence levels delta this  is called in the literature a multiple  delta estimator  and it's impossible in one dimension  finally just as a high-level comment  um this algorithm throws out log squared  or confidence number of samples which  contrasts with the optimal  one-dimensional algorithm from our fox  paper  in one dimension you have to throw out  log number of samples whereas here we're  throwing at log squared number of  samples and this gives some high level  sense of perhaps why our approach here  can't extend transparently to the low  dimensional case  our algorithm's error is one plus one  factor optimal it's intriguing that we  get such a tight algorithm algorithm  from a perhaps not so tight tail bound  i'll illustrate this with a diagram  so consider the probability distribution  of mean estimation error  so as we've discussed the ground truth  will adhere extremely tightly  to a spherical shell  what if  however we have a tail bound that  adheres to the spherical shell but maybe  not so tightly are we okay  well even though the red and the blue  curves differ a lot  in the y direction  what we care about is the delta  probability tails  and  the delta probability tail of the error  even though these two curves differ a  lot in the y direction is very similar  in the radial direction  in fact the ratio because both of these  are so close to the spherical shell is  going to be one plus one  this is why at a high level getting this  spherical shell tail bound is a big win  given this tail bound our algorithm is  really simple and it works for simple  robust reasons  the fact that this tailbone  has such a  simple and robust application here gives  us hope that perhaps its tailbone will  be useful in general  so  because this bound is the main tool of  the paper it's worth saying a few words  about its proof  the issue is that we're trying to  understand a tailbone on the radius of a  random vector in high dimensional space  well one very simple way to get started  is by taking one-dimensional turn-off  bounds in every direction x  so to draw a picture we're trying to  prove some bound on the probability of a  vector being outside of a sphere  and for any direction x we can just draw  a yellow line perpendicular to x and  compute  bound  the probability of um  being on on the far side of this yellow  line  we can do this for every x  it's not hard to check that if every  point outside the sphere is hit by a at  least a c fraction of these turn off  bounds then this  yields a bound of the probability of  being outside the sphere of  one over c times the average turnoff  bound  because c appears in the denominator  here this suggests that in fact it might  be to our advantage  to move this yellow line towards the  origin  so that each of these turn off regions  covers a bigger fraction of the sphere  we'll parameterize this by a distance  beta which we'll choose later  a second issue  is that  probability distributions in high  dimensions can be very spiky in the  sense that maybe they're supported only  on the axes  and if the turn off direction x is  aligned with the spike  then the bands that we get might blow up  badly  so one very simple way of  trying to address this is that  any support point y whose projection to  the direction x exceeds some threshold p  we just set aside and deal with  differently  so given beta and p these parameterize  our analysis  and simply optimize over beta and p to  get the best bound  so  while this may seem a bit unprincipled  um  and uh  it uses simple techniques  the reason why we get such a tight bound  is essentially the same reason that  turn-off bands are so tight the  exponential in the moment generating  function means typically very narrow  regions contribute most of the bound and  therefore it's enough to find a beta and  p that perform well on a narrow region  so  even though these techniques are fairly  direct and look fairly ad hoc  their good performance is essentially  the same reason that turn-off bands get  good performance and this optimization  technique is a  simple and natural approach that  yields the surprisingly tight and new  bounds that that drive our paper  so i'll end by briefly asking how tight  is our new vector tail bound  this bound is parameterized by  at least the three parameters  gamma the effective dimension and this  ratio between our radius bound and the  radius of the spherical shell  so  these three different parameters  convey that there's potentially a lot of  structure here  so to simplify this expression a little  bit  this  ratio  essentially falls into two regimes  depending upon which term in the  denominator is bigger so up to constant  factors in the exponent we can simplify  this as just gamma squared times the min  of these two things  so this is an upper bound to understand  how tight it is we need to understand  lower bounds this upper bound applies  for all distributions  the lower bound is going to come from a  single distribution  each lower band is going to come from a  single distribution whose tail we can  analyze  one distribution worth looking at is the  gaussian  and  as we pointed out the  gamma squared times the effective  dimension  portion of this upper bound essentially  represents the spherical shell behavior  of the gaussian so for this one regime  where where the effective dimension term  dominates here the performance of our  upper bound in fact matches that of the  gaussian  a different lower bound regime worth  considering  is a much spikier  less uniform distribution where the  distribution is supported only on the  axes and is far out  on the axes as our radius r bound will  allow  there's in fact  two sub-regimes of this that have rather  different behavior one is when the tail  is likely to be along a single axis  the other is when  the most extreme tail events are likely  to come in intermediate directions with  contributions from supports at many  support points at many different axes  so  our upper bound has these two different  regimes depending on which term  dominates and we have three different  regimes for lower bound  i'm going to draw a picture a diagram  that tries to convey what's going on  here  so  i'll depict the three-dimensional  parameter space  by projecting to a triangle where um  points near one vertex of the triangle  correspond to one of these three  different parameters being higher than  the other two  so  the two different parameter regimes of  the upper bound  are when the effective dimension is  bigger or smaller than the root trace  term which corresponds to being in the  left or the right side of the triangle  represented by this cross-hashed black  pattern here  meanwhile the three different lower  bounds  the regions in which each of them  dominate the other lower bounds are  depicted by these three colored regions  that match the three lower bounds  and  okay so  um  conveniently  the  crosshatch region  and the black region have the same bound  here  indicating that for the entire right  half of the triangle  our upper bound is in fact tight  on the left half though the situation is  much more complicated we have three  different lower bounds that each  dominate in different regions of the  triangle and none of these three  expressions match the corresponding  upper bound here gamma squared times  this root trace thing  so  our upper bound is tight for half the  parameter space and for half the  parameter space things seem rather  complicated  so i think it's worth looking at this  structure and  because it's  such a high dimensional parameter space  um this diagram hints that there's  actually a lot of struct a lot of  further structure worth understanding  with these new vector tail bands  contributions our main result today is a  simple linear time one plus little low  of one factor optimal mean estimation  algorithm that works in very high  dimensions  the tool we use to get this is a new  vector bernstein inequality capturing  the intuition that high dimensional  gaussians behave essentially like  spherical shells and extending this  intuition to the tails of arbitrary  bounded distributions  two new contributions which i haven't  mentioned  um one is  a new uh  tight one plus level of one factor lower  bound for mean estimation from an  algorithmic perspective  so  in the high dimensional regime this one  plus little of one  accurate analysis  hasn't really been discussed before  and  these lower bounds  in principle would be useful for future  work along this line  finally  the coordinate wise median of means  algorithm we use as a subroutine and we  have a new type analysis of this which  removes a spurious dependence on the  ambient dimension d  okay thanks
 

